    License: IETF Trust Legal Provisions; the examples are data.
    Format: the text of the RFC; the tests read the public key, private key and certificate of appendix C.
    Used: every example.

rfc9935.txt
    RFC 9935, "Internet X.509 Public Key Infrastructure - Algorithm Identifiers for the Module-Lattice-Based
    Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (ML-KEM)", https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9935.txt, downloaded on 2026-10-26.
    License: IETF Trust Legal Provisions; the examples are data.
    Format: the text of the RFC; the tests read the PEM blocks of appendix C with the heading they follow: the
    private keys of C.1 (seed, expanded and both, for ML-KEM-512, -768 and -1024), the public keys of C.2, the
    certificates of C.3 and the bad private keys of C.4, which must be rejected.
    Used: every example.

openssl/
    Keys, public keys, signatures and encrypted keys made by OpenSSL 3.5.7 (openssl-version.txt) in Debian trixie
    with make.sh, on 2026-10-26: an independent implementation the tests check against.
    License: generated data (OpenSSL is Apache License 2.0; its output is not covered).
    Format: NAME.key is a private key in PEM (PKCS #8, or PKCS #1 / RFC 5915 for rsa.pkcs1.key and p256.sec1.key,
    encrypted PKCS #8 with the password "umilib" for *.aes*-*.key), NAME.pub its SubjectPublicKeyInfo in PEM,
    NAME.ALGORITHM.sig the signature of message.txt in the form of a certificate's signatureValue.
    Used: every file.
